T60.d — Windows installer lifecycle candidate, 2026-11-10
The workflow selects only an unexpired xr-desktop-windows-x64 artifact belonging
to desktop-app.yml with the exact triggering SHA, records run ID and SHA, and
blocks/fails when none exists. On a Windows GitHub Actions runner, the candidate
script requires one MSI and one NSIS, rejects existing XR registration, installs
them separately, verifies app and sidecar, and launches the installed app.
It identifies its actual child sidecar by parent PID + installed path and its
loopback listening port; health must be 200, anonymous and bad bearer must be
401, the generated token must get 200 and the manifest version. Token is not
logged. It optionally screenshots, checks sidecar termination, uninstalls and
requires binary and registry removal plus a preserved isolated desktop-home
sentinel. This is not a live-provider, GUI manual, SmartScreen or cross-version
upgrade test. The first missing artifact or failed assertion prevents a pass.
Local PowerShell 7.5.4 was downloaded and SHA256-verified against its GitHub
release asset digest; parser OK. Negative test inputs missing/duplicated MSI or
NSIS fail before installer execution. PowerShell fakes test valid auth plus
four fault cases: anonymous accepted, invalid token accepted, valid token
rejected, wrong version. An early local control caught a collision with the
read-only PowerShell HOME variable; fixed by renaming the variable.
Local release + journey suites with PowerShell available: 331 pass, 0 fail.
Root TypeScript, CI policy, ownership, claim lint, and ledger checks passed.
None of this is native Windows installation evidence. The exact current-SHA
artifact must build, then this candidate must execute and its uploaded evidence
be inspected before any lifecycle task can be marked done.

Native Windows run 42914372905 on a62b54b0 correctly reported BLOCKED because
no exact-SHA Windows artifact existed, uploaded evidence artifact 13807699134,
and did not execute installers. Core CI run 42914382252 on the same SHA later
failed the first synthetic PowerShell input test: the log explicitly reports
Bun's 5-second test timeout; the evidence file did not yet exist. The other
three controls passed. Increased just these process-heavy test timeouts to 25s,
retaining the 15s child timeout, all assertions and a new explicit diagnostic
for child timeout/signal; no retry. Local controls 8 pass, 0 fail. CI rerun needed.

2026-11-10: push-triggered 42915394386 installer job 130179973797 failed
BLOCKED before any same-commit artifact had been uploaded. The prior lookup
also accepted pull-request desktop runs: GitHub may check out a synthetic merge
commit even when the run's head_sha names the PR head. The new prerequisite
waits at most one hour on an Ubuntu runner for an unexpired x64 artifact from
a desktop workflow_dispatch or push run with the exact head SHA. It refuses PR
runs, missing or duplicate artifacts, API failure, invalid SHA and timeout;
only then does the Windows lifecycle job start. A manual desktop dispatch on
the exact head is still necessary for this branch. No installer assertions
were weakened and artifact existence does not imply overall desktop CI success.
Local fake-gh cases ready/delayed/PR-only/other-SHA/missing/duplicate/API error
and invalid SHA pass; fake PowerShell controls pass. Combined targeted tests
12/0; full release suite 323/0 before the final PR-only mock strengthening;
root typecheck, CI policy and ownership pass. Native end-to-end test pending.

